siologically relevant  the major con to using primary cells  however is that generally they can only  be  cultured for short periods of time  believe it or not the success rate for  establishing a new cell line is still  relatively low even though these are  cancer cells  therefore you'd have to keep procuring  tumor tissue  or freshly isolated cancer cells  regularly to continue your primary cell  studies  one emerging cell  model type that bridges the gap between  these cancer  cell lines and primary cells  are our cancer are known as the cancer  organized model or tumoroids  these are freshly patient derived models  that are grown in 3d and may be cultured  for a relatively long period of time as  compared to primary cells grown in 2d  the culture method for these can be  relatively difficult but it may be worth  it for your research if you desire high  physiological relevance  one final note  rather than choosing one or the other  these can be used in tandem or  sequentially with your research starting  with cell lines to narrow the scope then  moving to primary cell culture later  for later studies for instance  and tumerics are not the only cells that  can be cultured in 3d of course cell  lines can also be cultured in 3d either  fully encapsulated in a basement  membrane extracts like gel tracks or  major gel or in suspension  but should you culture your cells in 3d  well it depends  both 2d and 3d culture have pros and  cons  2d culture is more widely utilized as it  is the easier of the two culture methods  it has a larger publication record with  which to contextualize one's research  and can be easier to monitor for  response to experimental conditions by  imaging for instance  however because adherent cell lines grow  in 2d  in a monolayer the cells are  experiencing a decidedly  non-physiological environment  they are attached to a plastic substrate  that is much stiffer than tissues in the  body  the cells are only interacting with each  other at the edges in two dimensions  and all the cells have free access to  nutrients in cell culture medium  cells in 3d on the other hand have  three dimensional cell cell interactions  do not interact much if at all  with a relatively stiff substrate  and the cells will have different access  to the nutrients from the medium  depending on where they are located  within the spheroid of cancer cells  however 3d cultures are notoriously  difficult to dissociate  and often  often utilize specialized protocols and  equipment for imaging  therefore depending on your research  question you may or may not want to  undertake 3d culture  or you may want to start with 2d culture  and progress to 3d at a later point  of course paramount to any of this  research is the type of medium that you  are feeding your cells  it is very important that you think  through  both the type of the basal medium  that you would like to use as well as  the supplements that you are planning on  using  and it really depends on your cell model  and your research question  the scientific literature is a good  place to start to find a medium but you  may want to adjust it for your specific  experiments  for instance if you are utilizing  estrogen receptor positive breast cancer  cell lines  you may want to use phenol red free  basal medium since phenol red is a weak  estrogen mimic  similarly a specialty fetal bovine serum  namely charcoal stripped fps  which has been treated to remove  hormones may also be useful for  estrogen-related studies  similarly you must carefully choose the  culture vessel or cell culture plastics  that are right for your purpose  first consider if you need your cells to  adhere or not this will help you choose  between non-treated or tissue  culture-treated vessels  of course you will also need to consider  if you are just scaling up your cultures  or if you are performing high throughput  experiments  if the latter health high throughput  meaning number of conditions or  replicates etc  do you need  also which type of assays will you be  utilizing are you looking at  fluorescence  you may want black wall plates  are you growing spheroids you may want  to try very low attachment plates like  nucleon sphera  and cell culture insert plate systems  have a variety of uses that we'll get  into in just a little bit  now back to our hallmarks of cancer  since we've gone over the cell models  and tools you may need for your cancer  research let's discuss how you can model  angiogenesis invasion and metastasis and  deregulating cellular energetics in  vitro  starting with angiogenesis  angiogenesis refers to the growth of new  blood vessels  this is key for cancer progression as  tumors can only grow so big without  being vascularized  if a tumor does not have its own blood  supply  running throughout  the cells at the center of the tumor  will start to die as nutrients will not  be able to reach them  therefore as seen in the video when  there is a small tumor forming or  breaking off from the primary site  it will secrete angiogenic growth  factors like vegf  in order to attract endothelial cells to  proliferate and infiltrate into the  tumor  thus allowing it to continue to grow  angiogenesis is also key for another  hallmark mark of cancer metastasis  as the tumor cells utilize blood vessels  to migrate from the primary site to the  to distant tissues for colonization  therefore cancer cell endothelial cell  interactions are very important to study  these types of studies can be done with  cell culture in several ways  for modeling angiogenesis in vitro  endothelial cells must be cultured with  an extracellular matrix mimic  or basement membrane extracts like  geltrex  in order to form  tube-like structures and networks  if you'd like to study the impact of  secreted  factor crosstalk between endothelial  cells and cancer cells  this can be accomplished with co-culture  and cell culture inserts  one could also look at  more direct contact by embedding  endothelial cells and cancer cells  together and an intuition mimic  more complex models may incorporate the  spatial relationship between cancer  cells within a tissue and endothelial  cells  lining a patent vessel which is best  modeled with microfluidic devices  wherein  we're in fluid flow reminiscent of blood  flow can also be accomplished  although angiogenesis and metastasis can  be observed in these complex  microfluidic devices  transient endothelial migration can also  be observed in a simpler setup  and we'll discuss that now  as cancer progresses cancer cells can  invade the local environment near the  primary tumor site and in some cases  migrate across the endothelial cell  barrier into the blood vessels in a  process called imtravisation  once inside the vasculature the cancer  cells can be carried to distant sites  far from the primary tumor and  extravasate or cross the endothelial  cell lining again  once more to colonize that distance site  and form a secondary tumor  the process of intravasation  extravasation and colonization is called  metastasis in the video you can see  cancer cells break away from the  extracellular matrix at the primary  tumor site and travis ate and  extravasate  to the secondary tumor site  invasion and metastasis can be studied  with cell culture in several ways  one of the simplest methods is the wound  healing assay where if you scratch a  monolayer of cells with a pipette tip  and see how long it takes to close the  gap in the monolayer  the rate at which the wound closes is  related to how migratory or invasive the  cells are either due to the cell type or  the condition to which they were exposed  in the figure below  we showed  show the increased wound healing  response of breast cancer cells with  exposure to heat stable basic fgf as  compared to a low sarah medium condition  a far more complex way to investigate  invasion and metastasis is with  microfluidic devices  where an endothelial cells can be seeded  to align a channel with  within a cancer cell laden extracellular  matrix mimic  and cancer cells can be observed  extravazating within the device  a classic and more widely used method  for modeling invasion however utilizes  cell culture inserts so let's take a  closer look at this approach  here we have diagrams showing the  classic setup for migration and invasion  experiments  for those that may not be familiar with  them cell culture inserts are  essentially smaller wells that fit  inside the larger wells of a well plate  this forms an upper chamber and a lower  chamber where media conditions or cells  can be seeded separately but can also  interact the interaction can occur  because the cell culture inserts have a  porous membrane for a bottom instead of  solid plastic  therefore for migration experiments  cancer cells can be seeded in the upper  chamber and a soluble factor that  attracts the cancer cells to migrate  towards it  known as a chemoattractant can be added  to the bottom chamber  if the cells are invasive or metastatic  they will migrate through the pores to  the underside of the membrane  where the number of migratory cells can  be counted  for invasion experiments the setup is  identical but before the cells are  seeded onto the forest membrane the  membrane is first coated in a basement  membrane extract like gel tracks  this adds yet another barrier that  invasive or metastatic cells must cross  to reach the chemoattractant on the  other side of the membrane  so let's look at an example of this in  action  for this experiment non-metastatic mcf7  cells and highly metastatic mda and b231  breast cancer cell lines were used  the chemo attractant was 10 fps  which was added only to the bottom  chamber  and the top chamber the cells proceeded  in serum free medium  after 20 hours the cells on the  underside of the porous membrane were  counted and the percent of cells that  migrated  was calculated  you can see that in both the migration  setup without gel tracks and the  invasion setup with gel trucks  a much higher percentage of the  metastatic mda mb231 cells cross the  membrane in response to fps as compared  to the non-metastatic mcf7 cell line  also interesting is that for the  migration setup  close to 90 percent of the mda and b231  cells made it across the membrane  versus only about 70 percent that made  it across and the invasion setup  this demonstrates the additional barrier  to migration that gel the gel trex  coding provides  so now that we understand the basics of  modeling cancer cell endothelial cell  interactions and invasion and metastasis  in the lab  let's take a look at a more complex  example  with this experiment an  extravasation event is modeled  if you look at the pains of the figure  from left to right the model gets more  and more complex  in panel a you just have the metastatic  prostate cell line pc3 the cells are  stained blue  and that are seated in the upper chamber  and the same medium  seated above and below the membrane  as there is no chemo attractant on the  bottom in the bottom chamber there is  little migration  next in panel b serum containing medium  was put in the into the bottom chamber  and just like in the previous example  the serum induced the pc3 cells to  migrate across the membrane  in panel c  instead of serum containing medium  in the bottom chamber bone cells known  as osteocytes were seeded in the bottom  and their secreted factors acted as a  chemoattractant to induce the pc3 cells  to migrate across the membrane  finally  in panel d you have the full  extravasation model  endothelial cells which are stained red  are seated on the underside of the  membrane to form a barrier  representative of the wall of a blood  vessel  again the osteocytes are used as the  chemoattractant  and amazingly you can see by the blue  on top of the red in the bottom image  that not only do the pc3 cells migrate  across the membrane but also across the  endothelial cell monolayer in response  to the soluble factors that the  osteocytes are secreting  this experiment is also a fantastic  example of how cell culture inserts can  be used first co-culture experiments  with a variety of cell types  the final hallmark of cancer we will  discuss today is deregulating cellular  energetics  this is a fairly complex topic but in  short  most normal cells and tissues utilize  mitochondria to get energy and only  switch to a secondary method of  metabolism known as glycolysis  if oxygen is in low supply because  glycolysis is a relatively inefficient  way to get energy  well in the 1920s otto warburg and  colleagues observed that cancer cells  were using far more glucose than the  surrounding normal cells they realize  that cancer cells often utilize  glycolysis to meet the high energy  requirements for tumor growth even in  oxygen-rich environments  this phenomenon was termed the warburg  effect  and it is a true hallmark of cancer  the increased glucose uptake of cancer  cells is even used in clinical imaging  to diagnose cancer  and monitor tumor response to therapy  cancer cell  metabolism not only has implications for  tumor growth but also responds to  anti-cancer drugs and is therefore very  important to model in a physiologically  relevant way in vitro  one tool that is very exciting to  accomplish this is  human plasma like medium or hplm  hplm was modeled after human plasma and  therefore is  more physiologically relevant than  traditional cell culture medium  for instance regarding the glucose which  was so critical to the warburg effect  hplm has about a third of the  concentration of glucose of other  traditional media formulations  as you might imagine this can have a  major impact on the metabolism of cancer  cells and their response to anti-cancer  drugs  in the data shown here you can see that  it took about 175 times the amount of  the cytotoxic compound styrosporine  to kill the same number of cells in  traditional dmemem medium as an hpl  lambdian  this may be explained by the unique  metabolic profile that cells in hplm  have versus traditional media  as shown in this next slide  for this study we have spheroid models  of three different cancer cell lines  each line was grown in three different  conditions  a traditional cell culture medium plus  regular fps  hplm plus regular fps and hplm plus  stylized fbs  you can see by the bar graphs that  depending on the media formulation and  the type of serum chosen  the cancer  cell metabolism as measured by atp  production is significantly different  therefore it is very important to  consider the formulation in which you  are growing or assaying yourselves  the closer we can match an invivo-like  environment to study cancer in the lab  the better our in-vitro results will  hold up when translated from the lab  bench to the bedside  so that wraps up our overview of how to  model in vitro  three of the hallmarks of cancer  outlined by hanahan and weinberg  hopefully this will inspire you to delve  into studying one or more of these  hallmarks in your own research  cancer is so common so complex and such  a difficult problem to solve  we can certainly use all the help we can  get to better prevent and treat this  awful disease in the years to come  for more information on the hallmarks of  cancer  how to choose your cancer cell models  and culture method  please check out our cancer cell culture  basics handbook at thermofisher.com  cancer cell culture  we also have a wealth of other valuable  information there including cancer cell  culture protocols and our  cell culture heroes some on our series  which is wonderful  and i invite you to check it out  i really appreciate your attendance and  attention today  and i'll be happy to take any questions  we have from the audience  thank you  thank you brittany for providing such  wonderful insights on her cancer cell  research we'll now start the live q a  portion of the webinar if you have a  question you like to ask please do so  now just click on the ask a question box  located on the far left of your screen  we'll answer as many of your questions  as we have time for  let's get started our first question is  on your slide on invasion and metastasis  i saw that heat stable basic fgf was  used why is heat stable basic fgf chosen  rather than native basic fgf  yeah that's a that's a great question so  native  human fgf  bfgf rapidly loses bioactivity at  standard culture conditions like 37  degrees celsius  this means that more frequent media  changes and more protein is required to  maintain biological activity  now heat stable fgf  is  specially engineered to be thermostable  and to retain bioactivity at  physiological temperatures  it has more than 90 sequence homology to  the native protein  but it has greater than  80 bioactivity after 72 hours at 37  degrees c so it's a very cool product  highly recommend heat staple  bfgf  thank you  the next question we have  just came through is which cancer cell  lines can i grow in gibco human plasma  like medium  yeah also also a very good question  so we've tested and shown compatibility  of hplm with a variety of cancer cell  lines representing  many different cancer types  breast cancer cell lines like mcf7 and  mda and b231  of course hela cells lincap a549 and  even jerkat  cells  use the hblm just like you would your  typical basal medium you supplement it  with fbs  and then you're good to go you don't  have to adapt it from your normal medium  at all  um even beyond cancer we've shown that  primary natural killer cells can also be  grown in hplm  so if you're interested in cancer immune  co-culture studies you can utilize hplm  there as well  we do have a full list of all the cancer  cell lines and other cell types that  we've tested  on our website  great thank you and looks like we have  time for just one more question here  i've noticed that thermal fissure offers  a variety of cell culture inserts for  invasion and migration studies  how do i choose which one to order yeah  yeah you have to be careful so  the biggest difference in the cell  culture inserts is the pore size  so different applications and cell types  require different pore sizes  the first step is to determine what pore  size you need for your for your study of  interest  uh if it's a smaller pore size like 0.4  microns  this would be for applications where you  don't want the cells to migrate through  the pores so this would be just for  co-culture or transport type studies for  just secreted factors or going above and  below the membrane  uh the larger pore sizes like eight  microns it's what's recommended for  studies where you would like to see the  cells migrate  chemotaxis invasion studies  i would recommend checking out the  application notes on our website and  that should help guide you into which  cell culture insert product would be  appropriate for your study of interest  okay  well this wraps up our git code galaxy  tour webinar today i would like to thank  brittany for sharing her cell culture  experience in cancer research which i'm  sure will be very beneficial to all of  the cancer cell researchers a big thank  you to our audience for joining us today  and submitting interesting questions for  those questions that we are unable to  answer today we'll follow up with an  answer via email  we hope all of you have gained more  knowledge today  that will help you enhance your lab  skills  we'd also like to thank lab roots and  our sponsor thermal fisher scientific  for underwriting today's educational  webcast  before we go i'd like to ask any  everyone any and everyone to take our  survey at the end of this webinar  just so you can tell us your feedback  when you complete the survey you will be  entered into a drawing to win a gibco  exclusive lego set and a featured  product from this webinar this webcast  can be viewed on demand  lab roots will alert you via email when  it's available for replay we encourage  you to share that email with your  colleagues who may have missed today's  live event  hope to see you again for our next gibco  galaxy tour webinar focusing on genome  editing in october thank you  and goodbye for now  you
 

